# Target C-II — double-unit corner / flat-center overlap

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** teeth-versus-double-unit-corner squeeze; bridge-subtracted outer-face isoperimetry; low-`t` pentagon sea; empty-pentagon trapezoid stability; minimax atomic gap `0.0021`
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement awaiting independent audit
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current boundary theorem forces either many outer two-edge teeth or many exact
double-unit loaded hull corners. Its degree-two double-unit branch was previously
recorded only as loaded contact-degree slack. In the small true-residual regime that
loses geometric information: except for an explicitly paid set of bridge, high-degree,
and high-residual incidences, a small-turn degree-two double-unit corner is the flat
middle vertex of an actual pentagonal exceptional face.

Let

- `N_2` be the genuine two-edge outer-tooth count;
- `C_11` be the number of loaded hull vertices whose two adjacent loaded-hull sides
  both have length exactly one;
- `B_o` be the number of outer-face graph bridges;
- `X:=Q-f=sum_{d>=6}(d-5)n_d` be exceptional-face degree excess above pentagons;
- `R_F` be the true force-hull residual;
- `L_flat^bd` be the set of loaded flat-pentagon centers produced below; and
- `E_in^(4)` be the distinct third contacts at degree-three `C_11` corners whose
  exterior turn is smaller than `pi/4`.

There is a fixed residual threshold `theta_*>0` such that every sufficiently large
finite source obeys

\[
\boxed{
N_2+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}^{(4)}|+2B_o+6X+{5R_F\over\theta_*}
\ge 2q-P_F-3d_{out}-8.
}
\tag{OF.1}
\]

Here `P_F=per(F)` and `d_out=b-P_F`. Every edge in `E_in^(4)` is a loaded-to-unloaded
unit contact and has inward angle-bisector component greater than `sqrt(3)/2`.

On a convergent low-`t` Type-II subsequence with

\[
t={q-k\over D},\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},\qquad R_F=o(D),
\]

put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\qquad
C_{21}:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0021.
\]

Then bridge-subtracted isoperimetry and the low-`t` degree-excess bound turn (OF.1)
into

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}^{(4)}|\over D}
\ge
5\pi+3C_{21}-{23\over2}t+2\delta+{7\over2}\kappa.
}
\tag{OF.2}
\]

In particular,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_2+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}^{(4)}|\over D}
\ge
5\pi+3C_{21}-{23\over2}t.
}
\tag{OF.3}
\]

The right side stays positive for

\[
t<{2\over23}(5\pi+3C_{21})=1.8633165312\ldots .
\]

At the current proof-only endpoint

\[
t_{21}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right)+0.0017279
=1.82124143797944\ldots,
\]

(OF.3) gives

\[
\boxed{
{N_2+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}^{(4)}|\over D}
\ge0.483863572369096\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{OF.4}
\]

and hence

\[
\boxed{
\max\{N_2,|L_{flat}^{bd}|,|E_{in}^{(4)}|\}
\ge0.161287857456365\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{OF.5}
\]

Even uniformly on `t<=1.8585`, (OF.3) remains positive and gives the corresponding
three-way maximum floor `0.0184633697109034...D-o(D)`.

The gain is structural rather than numerical: the old `s_B` escape has been replaced
by actual loaded flat pentagon centers, placing that branch directly inside the global
flat-center stress/activation trichotomy.

## 1. Small-turn double-unit corners

Let `A,B,C` be consecutive loaded force-hull vertices with

\[
|A-B|=|B-C|=1.
\]

Write `phi_B` for the exterior turn at `B`. Since the total exterior turn of the
convex force hull is `2pi`, at most eight loaded hull vertices satisfy

\[
\phi_B\ge\pi/4.
\tag{OF.6}
\]

Restrict to `C_11` corners with `phi_B<pi/4`. The hull interior angle at `B` is then

\[
\theta_B=\pi-\phi_B>3\pi/4.
\tag{OF.7}
\]

The loaded contact-degree bound gives `deg_C(B)=2` or `3`.

If `deg_C(B)=3`, let `BW` be the third contact. The double-unit-corner theorem already
proves that `W` is unloaded and that `BW` points strictly into the hull with normal
component at least `sqrt(3)/2`. Different corners have different loaded endpoints, so
these selected third-contact edges are distinct. They form `E_in^(4)`.

It remains to understand the degree-two corners.

## 2. Degree two plus no bridge gives one bounded face

Suppose `deg_C(B)=2` and neither `AB` nor `BC` is an outer-face bridge. Since `B` is
on the convex hull and has only the two incident contact edges, its rotation has two
face sectors: the exterior sector and the hull-interior sector between `BA` and `BC`.
The exterior sector is the unbounded face. Each nonbridge boundary edge has a bounded
face on its other side, so the two interior-side face incidences must be the same
bounded face; call it `P_B`.

If at least one of `AB,BC` is an outer bridge, charge this corner to such a bridge.
Every bridge has only two endpoints, so the number of degree-two `C_11` corners lost
in this way is at most

\[
2B_o.
\tag{OF.8}
\]

This count is by physical edges and remains valid when the outer-face walk repeats
vertices or bridge occurrences.

## 3. The bounded face cannot be quadrilateral

Every bounded contact face has unit boundary sides. If `P_B` had degree four, it
would be a convex equilateral quadrilateral, hence a unit rhombus. If `theta` is one
of its interior angles, its two diagonals have lengths

\[
2\sin(\theta/2),\qquad 2\cos(\theta/2).
\]

Unit separation and triangle-freeness make both nonadjacent distances strictly larger
than one. Therefore

\[
\pi/3<\theta<2\pi/3.
\tag{OF.9}
\]

But the angle of `P_B` at `B` is the whole hull-interior sector and hence equals
`theta_B>3pi/4`, contradicting (OF.9). Thus

\[
\deg P_B\ge5.
\tag{OF.10}
\]

So every remaining degree-two small-turn double-unit corner is incident to an
exceptional bounded face.

## 4. Pay the nonpentagons exactly by `6X`

Let `n_d` be the number of bounded exceptional faces of degree `d`. For `d>=6`,

\[
d\le6(d-5).
\]

Hence the total number of boundary-vertex incidences on nonpentagonal exceptional
faces is at most

\[
\sum_{d\ge6}d n_d
\le6\sum_{d\ge6}(d-5)n_d
=6X.
\tag{OF.11}
\]

Deleting all degree-two corners whose `P_B` has degree at least six therefore costs at
most `6X` corners, with occurrence counting already built into the face degrees.

Every remaining corner is incident to a pentagonal exceptional face.

## 5. Cheap pentagons identify the corner as the flat center

At the zero-residual pentagon endpoint the unique equality shape is the flat unit
trapezoid. Its five interior angles are, up to cyclic order,

\[
\pi,\quad {2\pi\over3},\quad {2\pi\over3},\quad {\pi\over3},\quad {\pi\over3}.
\tag{OF.12}
\]

Choose a fixed trapezoid-stability neighborhood small enough that every non-middle
vertex of a retained pentagon has interior angle strictly below `3pi/4`. Combine its
residual threshold with the first Wegner residual jump, and call the resulting positive
constant `theta_*`.

All local exceptional-face residuals are nonnegative and their sum is at most `R_F`.
Thus the number of pentagons with residual at least `theta_*` is at most
`R_F/theta_*`. Each such pentagon has five boundary incidences, so deleting all
high-residual pentagon-corner incidences costs at most

\[
{5R_F\over\theta_*}.
\tag{OF.13}
\]

For every retained pentagon, the angle at `B` is larger than `3pi/4` by (OF.7), while
all four non-middle angles are smaller than `3pi/4`. Therefore `B` is exactly the
distinguished almost-flat middle vertex. It is loaded by construction, so `B` belongs
to the loaded flat-center class from the global stress trichotomy. Distinct corners
are distinct source vertices, so no multiplicity loss remains.

Consequently, among the small-turn `C_11` corners,

\[
\boxed{
|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}^{(4)}|
\ge C_{11}-8-2B_o-6X-{5R_F\over\theta_*}.
}
\tag{OF.14}
\]

## 6. Combine with the exact tooth/corner squeeze

The finite boundary theorem gives

\[
N_2+C_{11}\ge2q-P_F-3d_{out}.
\tag{OF.15}
\]

Substituting (OF.14) proves the finite burden (OF.1).

This step is the main point: degree-two double-unit corners are not left as one unit of
abstract loaded contact-degree slack. After explicit error payments they become
actual loaded flat centers.

## 7. Low-`t` normalization

For the canonical full positive-contact core, the bounded-face union has asymptotic
area `pi D^2/4`. Bridge-subtracted outer-face isoperimetry therefore gives, on a
convergent subsequence,

\[
\limsup {2B_o\over D}\le\delta.
\tag{OF.16}
\]

The audited low-`t` identities with the minimax atomic gap `0.0021` give

\[
x:={X\over D}
\le {9t\over4}-\pi-{C_{21}\over2}-\delta-{\kappa\over4}+o(1).
\tag{OF.17}
\]

Divide (OF.1) by `D`, use `q/D=t+kappa`, `P_F/D->pi`, `R_F/D->0`, then apply
(OF.16)--(OF.17):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\liminf {N_2+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}^{(4)}|\over D}
&\ge 2t+2\kappa-\pi-4\delta-6x\\
&\ge 5\pi+3C_{21}-{23\over2}t
       +2\delta+{7\over2}\kappa.
\end{aligned}
\]

This is (OF.2); dropping the nonnegative bonuses gives (OF.3).

## 8. Optional conversion of the tooth branch

For every fixed admissible tooth threshold `eta`, the shallow-tooth penetration theorem
gives

\[
4E_{pen}(\eta)\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
\tag{OF.18}
\]

Hence (OF.2) also implies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {4E_{pen}(\eta)+|L_{flat}^{bd}|+|E_{in}^{(4)}|\over D}
\ge
5\pi+3C_{21}-{23\over2}t
+\left(2-{1\over\eta}\right)\delta+{7\over2}\kappa.
}
\tag{OF.19}
\]

At the endpoint profile `t=t_21`, using `delta<=0.002837775` and `eta=0.06`, the
right side is at least

\[
0.442242872369096\ldots .
\tag{OF.20}
\]

Thus at the current endpoint one of the three actual source structures
`E_pen(0.06)`, `L_flat^bd`, `E_in^(4)` has density at least
`0.0737071453948493...D-o(D)`, where both edge families consist of strongly inward
unit contacts and the middle alternative is a loaded flat-pentagon-center population.

## 9. Scope audit

- `C_11` consists of exact unit loaded-hull sides; no near-unit side is promoted to a
  contact.
- The common bounded face in Section 2 uses only degree two, convex-hull sidedness, and
  the definition of an outer bridge.
- The quadrilateral exclusion uses strict unit separation of the two rhombus diagonals;
  equality would already create a forbidden unit equilateral triangle.
- The `6X` payment is an incidence bound, so one high-degree face may pay several bad
  corners without undercounting.
- The `5R_F/theta_*` payment likewise counts pentagon incidences rather than just
  pentagons.
- The bridge estimate (OF.16) uses the canonical full-core near-isodiametric bounded
  face union; it is not asserted for an arbitrary selected subgraph.
- The normalized theorem additionally assumes the true small-residual branch
  `R_F=o(D)`.
- The numerical constants inherit the proof-only minimax-atomic-gap lifecycle.
- No exact unit equilateral triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` competitor has
  yet been produced.

**Disposition:** new proof-only structural candidate. C-II and Level I remain open.
